    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Over 45 million youth in the US participate in sports, yet conversations about emotional health in athletics are rare.  The intersection of athletics with race, gender, class, body image, mental health, sexual harassment/assault, and many other facets of identity continue to go unspoken about and young athletes are forced to navigate these challenges alone. A number of social-emotional focused sports programs highlight the relationship between positive social-emotional development and sports. However, none of them address the  potential challenges that sports culture can oftentimes pose for adolescents, depriving young athletes of critical opportunities to grow emotionally.\r\n\r\nBeyond the Game is a digital training program and curriculum that prepares coaches of adolescent sports teams with the tools, resources, and confidence they need to facilitate open, honest, and healthy conversations about emotional health from the context of sports. The program targets coaches of young adolescents from ages 9-15, drawing from research by Ronald Dahl (2004) and Gregory Walton (2014) that demonstrates the capability of wise interventions for adolescents to produce lasting impact. The platform leverages the power of role models in the form of older athletes and ex-athletes to start the conversation by sharing personal narratives. An early stage prototype of this product was tested with two youth basketball coaches who showed increased awareness of sports culture and its potential negative impact on young athletes, placed increased value on emotional health in sports, and came away from the experience prepared to start a conversation about emotional health with their teams.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
